exports[`the concepts prompt, a redo: the previous version and the comment, literal 1`] = `
"You are working on a mockup: «MTC app», for Medical Trust Center. This stage is the CONCEPTS: from the approved brief, propose two or three different angles for the mockup, so the owner can choose one.\\n\\n## Where things are\\n\\n- The mockup folder, in the owner's Obsidian vault (the documents go here): /Users/me/Obsidian/01-projects/medical-trust-center/mockup\\n- The work folder (inputs and code): /Users/me/BWR/Projects/MedicalTrustCenter\\n- The inputs: /Users/me/BWR/Projects/MedicalTrustCenter/inputs\\n\\n- MTC APP.pdf\\n- notas-junta.docx\\n- notes-1.md (the owner’s note)\\n\\n## Rules\\n\\n- Write every document in the language of the inputs (if they are in Spanish, the documents are in Spanish).\\n- Do NOT open anything in /Users/me/BWR/Projects/MedicalTrustCenter/inputs/private: it is real client data, and only the plan stage may look at it.\\n- Word files: read them with \`textutil -convert txt -stdout <file>\` in the shell.\\n- PDF files: read them with your file reading tool. A PDF with no text layer (a scan) still renders its pages: read it as an image. Only if you still cannot read it, put that in the open questions.\\n- Every document starts with a YAML frontmatter block.\\n- Do not invent what the inputs do not say: what you infer is an assumption, and is written as one.\\n\\n## This is a redo\\n\\nThe owner reviewed the previous version and asked for changes. The previous version was copied here:\\n\\n- /Users/me/Obsidian/01-projects/medical-trust-center/mockup/_versions/02-concepts-v1.md\\n\\nThe owner’s comment, literal:\\n\\n> Un tercer ángulo, más sobrio.\\n\\nRewrite the document in place, starting from the previous version and doing what the comment asks. Keep what the comment does not touch.\\n\\n## What to read\\n\\nThe approved brief: /Users/me/Obsidian/01-projects/medical-trust-center/mockup/01-brief.md. It is the source of truth; go back to the inputs only to check a detail.\\n\\n## What to write\\n\\nOne file: /Users/me/Obsidian/01-projects/medical-trust-center/mockup/02-concepts.md\\n\\nFrontmatter: \`title\`, \`type: mockup-concepts\`, \`client\`, \`created\` (today, YYYY-MM-DD) and \`options\` (the ids of the concepts, in order, on one line: \`options: [a, b, c]\`).\\n\\nThen 2 or 3 concepts, each under its own heading \`## <id> — <title>\`, where the id is a short lower-case slug (\`a\`, \`b\`, \`c\` is fine). Each concept says:\\n\\n1. **The line** — the idea in one sentence, as the owner would say it out loud.\\n2. **For whom** — who it speaks to (someone on the client's side, or the users of the owner's own project, as the brief tells) and what they care about.\\n3. **The step that would sell it** — ONE step of the demo, the moment it clicks (the brief marks one; a concept may move it).\\n4. **Risk** — what could go wrong with this angle, or what the brief does not settle for it.\\n\\nThe concepts must be really different angles, not three wordings of the same one. Keep to what the brief says the mockup must show; nothing out of scope comes back in.\\n\\nWhen you call \`submit_stage\`, pass \`options\`: one per concept, with the same \`id\` and \`title\` as in the document and a one-sentence \`blurb\` (the line). 2 or 3 options, never more.\\n\\n## When you are done\\n\\nCall the tool \`submit_stage\` with runId \`0192f3a4-5b6c-7d8e-9f01-23456789abcd\`, files [\\"02-concepts.md\\"] and a one-sentence summary. If it refuses, fix what it says and call it again. When it accepts, end your turn: the owner reviews it from the phone."
`;

